x = {child: model.addVar(vtype=COPT.BINARY, name=f"x_{child}") for child in children}

# Set the objective function
model.setObjective(cp.quicksum(costs[child] * x[child] for child in children), sense=COPT.MINIMIZE)

# Add constraints
model.addConstr(cp.quicksum(x[child] for child in children) <= 3, name="MaxChildren")
model.addConstr(x["Bob"] == 1, name="TakeBob")
model.addConstr(x["Alice"] + x["Diana"] <= 1, name="NoAliceAndDiana")
model.addConstr(x["Bob"] + x["Charlie"] <= 1, name="NoBobAndCharlie")
model.addConstr(x["Charlie"] <= x["Diana"], name="TakeCharlieAndDiana")
model.addConstr(x["Diana"] <= x["Ella"], name="TakeDianaAndElla")
model.addConstr(cp.quicksum(x[child] for child in children) >= 2, name="MinChildren")

# Solve the model
model.solve()

# Output the results
if model.status == COPT.OPTIMAL:
    print("Minimum total cost: ${}".format(model.objval))
    print("Children to take:")
    for child in children:
        if x[child].x == 1:
            print(child)
else:
    print("No optimal solution found.")
```

This script first defines the children and their costs, then creates a binary decision variable for each child to represent whether they are taken. The objective function is set to minimize the total cost.

Next, the script adds all the constraints mentioned in the problem description. These constraints ensure that the solution satisfies the maximum number of children, the decision to take Bob, the exclusion of taking Alice and Diana together, the exclusion of taking Bob and Charlie together, the requirement to take Diana if Charlie is taken, the requirement to take Ella if Diana is taken, and the decision to take at least two children.

Finally, the script solves the model and outputs the minimum total cost and the children to take (if an optimal solution is found).

